+# Dated security disposition â€” 2026-09-28
+
+Decision: NOT CLEARED. This assessment identifies affected pins and missing evidence; it is not a blanket waiver or a claim of exhaustive native-package clearance. No pins changed. No package downloaded/installed or container executed. Advisory JSON and upstream release HTML were retrieved as inert evidence. Stage A preserved; Stage B paused; pilots unauthorized.
+
+## Exact scope and evidence
+
+Base python@sha256:ac71103cf5137882806aad2d7ece409bbfe86c075e7478752d36ea073b0934d7, config sha256:e6d8b768c22ff169d0d5b7449ecede9ff35f2cf7f11f401df313e5d57e28c7a4; CPython3.10.17 Debian12 amd64. The reviewed inventory has 105 native binary-package records and embedded pip23.0.1/setuptools65.5.1/wheel0.45.1. All are enumerated in security-coverage.json; unresolved rows are not called safe. The 13 locked wheels and exact-version GitHub advisory query results are also enumerated there. Raw response URLs, UTC retrieval time and SHA-256 are in security-sources-live/receipt.json. No pagination may be ignored; this retrieval showed no next pages. Initial denied network attempts are retained separately, not mistaken for successful queries.
+
+## Affected versions and path applicability
+
+- torch2.10.0+cpu: GHSA-rrmf-rvhw-rf47 / CVE-2025-3000 affects <=2.12.1; fix2.13.0. The reviewed guard disables torch.jit.script before torchvision import, but imports/native paths still execute. No proof of non-reachability and no full-package mitigation claim. Separate risk decision required. https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-rrmf-rvhw-rf47
+- CVE-2025-32434 fixed at torch2.6 and CVE-2026-24747 fixed at2.10: pinned2.10 meets those specific historical fix floors, not all advisories.
+- pip23.0.1: seven exact-version matches retained in pip-13.json. GHSA-qwm4-qh6w-59xr (<26.2.0; double-encoded index URLs); GHSA-wf93-45jw-7689 (<26.1.2; wheel entrypoint traversal); GHSA-jp4c-xjxw-mgf9 (<26.1; untrusted functionality); GHSA-58qw-9mgm-455v (<=26.0.1; tar/ZIP ambiguity); GHSA-6vgw-5pg2-w6jp (<26.0; path traversal); GHSA-4xh5-x5gv-qwph (<=25.2; tar symlink extraction); GHSA-mq26-g339-26xf (<23.3; Mercurial command injection). Offline hash-bound wheels/no index/no VCS/no sdists reduce several paths; wheel installation IS exercised, so entrypoint/archive issues cannot be dismissed without inspecting the exact wheel metadata/member paths. That inspection remains a blocker. No pip upgrade is silently made.
+- Embedded setuptools65.5.1: GHSA-h35f-9h28-mq5c (<83; macOS normalization), GHSA-5rjg-fvgr-3xxf (<78.1.1; PackageIndex path traversal), GHSA-cx63-2mw6-8hw5 (<70; URL command injection). Linux/no sdist/no online PackageIndex avoids the intended workflows, and lock installs84.0.0. Old bytes nevertheless exist during installation; this is bounded exposure, not an assertion they never import.
+- wheel0.45.1: GHSA-8rrh-rw8j-w5fx affects0.40.0â€“0.46.1; fix0.46.2; wheel-unpack path traversal/permissions. Proposed command does not invoke wheel unpack, but pip's separate wheel handling still requires the above assessment.
+- Remaining twelve locked distributions: no match returned by the exact pip advisory queries on this date. This is database-limited evidence, not absence of vulnerabilities. Includes locked setuptools84.0.0. All versions are in security-coverage.json.
+- CPython3.10.17 predates3.10.18 fixes for CVE-2024-12718, CVE-2025-4138/4330/4435/4517 tar extraction bypasses, unicode-escape use-after-free and ipaddress resource exhaustion. This workflow does not intentionally extract tar with target Python; Docker handles OCI layers and pip consumes wheels. Exact Python runtime/module reachability is not fully proved. Later3.10.19/20/21 release records are preserved for review; being in the3.10 series does not make3.10.17 fully patched. https://www.python.org/downloads/release/python-31018/
+- openssl/libssl3 3.0.16-1~deb12u1 is below Debian's CVE-2025-9230 fix3.0.17-1~deb12u3. CMS password-recipient decryption is not an intended build/inspection operation; network disabled during those operations. This is a path-specific observation, not package clearance. https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2025-9230
+- libc6 2.36-9+deb12u10 is below Debian's CVE-2025-4802 fix2.36-9+deb12u11. Exploit requires statically linked setuid executable performing dlopen with untrusted LD_LIBRARY_PATH. No-new-privileges and no injected LD_LIBRARY_PATH mitigate this scenario; exact native executable coverage remains unverified. https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2025-4802
+
+## Native coverage limitation and exact outstanding evidence
+
+The supplied inventory lists binary package names/versions, not complete binary-to-source mappings. Debian's source-indexed bulk response exceeded the collector's40MiB safety bound, so it was rejected rather than treated as an empty vulnerability set. 105 native rows have explicit unresolved dispositions (including the two concrete affected families above). To complete full native review, obtain hash-bound source-package mappings for that exact base and scoped Debian advisory records (including backport/fixed versions) for all mapped packages; or explicitly review a replacement base as a separate pin change. No blanket accept-all is proposed. Host kernel/Docker vulnerabilities also require evaluation against the actual future runner version, not only the previous VM's metadata.
+
+## Actions dependencies
+
+actions/checkout v4.2.2 @11bd71901bbe5b1630ceea73d27597364c9af683; upload-artifact v4.6.2 @ea165f8d65b6e75b540449e92b4886f43607fa02. Fresh exact Actions-ecosystem advisory queries returned no entries. This does NOT clear embedded npm dependencies or runner-provided Node; those bundled dependency inventories were not audited here and are an explicit outstanding requirement. Manual trusted branch only, no pull_request_target/fork events, contents:read, persist-credentials:false, no secrets into containers, reports-only upload, one-day retention mitigate workflow attack paths. Upload runtime token remains a capability on the host. Pinning establishes identity, not safety. https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/secure-use
+
+## Build method amendment and residual lifecycle risk
+
+The unbounded anonymous legacy Docker build is replaced by a named, labeled ordinary container running ONLY offline pip install/check. It has one CPU,4GiB RAM/memsw,16 tasks, no network, no capabilities, no-new-privileges, default Docker seccomp; inspected HostConfig must match. Root/writable layer is restricted to the build container, necessary for installation; it is NOT the inspector runtime profile. No host bind or Docker socket enters the build container. Inspector runtime custom seccomp/read-only/nonroot guards are unchanged. Build log rotation,240/30s internal subprocess deadlines,300s external polling,10s control calls,60s copy/snapshot calls, exact-name kill/inspect/remove and an independent always cleanup step replace CLI-only termination. Signals preserve a primary error and attempt cleanup. No SIGKILL/VM-loss guarantee is asserted.
+
+IMPORTANT remaining blocker: Docker image pull/copy/commit are daemon operations. A CLI deadline does not prove those operations terminated; snapshot timeout is explicitly reported unconfirmed. Stopped installation-container termination is checked independently. To claim an end-to-end hard daemon-I/O bound requires a disposable daemon/VM teardown control with verifiable completion, or another reviewed snapshot/build transport. GitHub hosted VM teardown is provider-managed, not independently evidenced by this workflow. Image layer storage also lacks a per-build hard byte quota. Thus bounded installation COMPUTE is implemented/tested with mocks, but full build-I/O lifecycle clearance is still blocked. Do not dispatch on the strength of passing mocked tests.
+
+Approval fields remain false. The next decision must resolve the exact old pip/wheel/Python/native findings, Actions transitive review and daemon-I/O lifecycle control; changing versions or accepting residual risk requires explicit review. No research loading authorization is created.
